---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/format';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/makaipremi/thesis-tracker';
---

<Base title="Verify">
	<div class="prose">
		<h1>Verify it yourself</h1>
		<p class="lead">
			You shouldn't have to trust me. Here's how to check, independently, that a thesis was written when it says, hasn't
			been changed since, and that its numbers are right.
		</p>

		<h2>1. Was it edited after posting?</h2>
		<p>
			Every thesis page links to <strong>"Full edit history on GitHub"</strong>. It lists every change ever made to that
			thread's files. New information goes in new replies rather than rewriting old posts, so normally you'll see one
			commit adding each post and nothing changing it afterwards. If a post was ever changed, for example to fix a typo,
			that commit is listed too; click it to see exactly what changed.
		</p>

		<h2>2. When was it written?</h2>
		<p>
			Git dates can be set to anything on my own computer, so the real anchor is <strong>OpenTimestamps</strong>. When
			a post is published, its SHA-256 fingerprint is submitted to OpenTimestamps and, within a few hours, recorded in
			the Bitcoin blockchain. Nobody — including me — can create a proof with an earlier date.
		</p>
		<p>To check a post:</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				Under each post on a thesis page, download the <strong>proof (.ots)</strong> and the <strong>post file</strong>.
				Proofs live in the <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/proofs`}><code>proofs/</code></a> folder, one per version of each
				post, named by the file's SHA-256 fingerprint.
			</li>
			<li>
				Go to <a href="https://opentimestamps.org">opentimestamps.org</a>, drop in the <code>.ots</code> file, then the
				post file when asked.
			</li>
			<li>
				It should say the file existed as of a Bitcoin block and date. That date should be on or just after the post's
				date. If a post was ever edited, each earlier version has its own proof; get that version of the file from the
				GitHub history to check it. If the file had been changed by even one character, verification fails.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p class="muted">
			Command-line alternative: <code>pip install opentimestamps-client</code>, then
			<code>ots verify -f post.md proof.ots</code>. A proof less than a few hours old shows as pending until Bitcoin confirms it.
		</p>

		<h2>3. Are the numbers right?</h2>
		<p>
			Every price used is in the public <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/prices`}><code>prices/</code></a> folder, one file per
			ticker, saved when fetched and never overwritten. To check a return:
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				Look up the same daily closes on Yahoo Finance's historical data page. Use the <strong>Close</strong> column, not
				Adj Close; dividends and splits are applied separately and listed in the same file.
			</li>
			<li>
				For a single long position: total return = (close at the end, plus reinvested dividends) ÷ entry close − 1. A
				short is the opposite sign.
			</li>
			<li>
				For a pair or basket: add up each leg's return × its size (negative for shorts), then divide by the total of all
				sizes.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Every thesis page also has a <strong>data table</strong> under its chart listing the daily return, so you can check
			any date.
		</p>

		<h2>What can't be verified</h2>
		<p>
			These checks prove that a posted thesis is genuine and unaltered. They can't prove I haven't kept other ideas to
			myself — only that nothing posted has been changed or removed. See the <a href={url()}>methodology</a>.
		</p>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	.prose {
		max-width: 44rem;
	}
	.lead {
		font-size: 1.15rem;
	}
	h2 {
		font-size: 1.15rem;
		margin: 2rem 0 0.5rem;
	}
	li {
		margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
	}
	code {
		font-size: 0.9em;
	}
</style>
